    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nClicking somewhere with `<LeftMouse>` does not remove extra cursors.\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/ad15ce8e-05c7-43a9-a188-7a22f4611f22\n\n### Expected behavior\n\n`<LeftMouse>` removes all extra cursors (like `<C-l>`), like other editors do.\n\nHelix:\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/93ff1b8e-6c14-420e-86a1-4fd2d587b4de\n\nVSCode:\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/72f72b96-88cc-492c-87db-b02552d17417\n\nJetbrains:\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/37763d62-e0b8-4c40-b85a-95123219c897",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "I'm hesitant to add many ways that cursors are implicitly removed; an accidental click, jump, etc., should not be \"punished\"? It's easy to remove cursors if that is the intention. OTOH, if the accidental input causes a destructive (cascaded) edit, that is something to consider...\n\nI did reference other IDEs (zed, helix, vscode, sublime). But my sense is that they do backflips to avoid confusing users who aren't aware that multicursors even exist. The multicursor interface of many of those editors is actually quite confusing.",
